hello i'm caslin fields and i'm a  developer advocate at google cloud  today i'll be walking through the basics  to get started with anthocon bare metal  let's get going  anthos is a platform made up of a  variety of products  all designed with enterprise use cases  in mind one key factor of enterprise  ecosystems is that they often span  hybrid and multi-cloud environments the  enthos platform is designed to give a  consistent user experience across  platforms  you can expect that products with anthos  in the name will have features and  capabilities designed for hybrid and  multi-cloud environments  today i'll be talking about anthoconbear  metal  which is a tool you can use to  incorporate your on-prem environments  into the anthos platform  anthocon bare metal gives you the google  provided consistent experience  and useful tools and features of anthos  for you to run on your own  self-managed hardware we know that every  environment is different  so there are a variety of supported  configurations which you can learn more  about in the documentation  the install process with anthocon bare  metal includes an  integrated preflight check to ensure  your system meets requirements  the installer itself allows you to  declaratively deploy anthos  components onto your own on-prem systems  anthocon bare metal can be deployed on  either physical or  virtual infrastructure and supports a  variety of linux distributions  such as red hat enterprise linux centos  or ubuntu anthocon bare metal comes with  an  out of the box kubernetes networking  stack including load balancing and  overlay networking  there are some basic requirements around  networking for the hardware you're using  to run your anthocon bare metal cluster  which you can learn more about in the  documentation  integration with the container storage  interface allows for integration with  your on-prem storage infrastructure  anthocon bare metal provides tooling to  perform smooth  in-place upgrades and includes  out-of-the-box integration with cloud  operations  formerly known as stackdriver for system  as well as application logs and metrics  as well as the potential to integrate  with other observability tools  while anthos is largely centered around  kubernetes we know not  every workload may be able to run in  containers so enthos can also make use  of  cubevert cubevert is an open source  project allowing kubernetes to manage  virtual machines  like it manages containers there are a  number of prerequisites your environment  will need to meet  in order to be used with anthocon bare  metal  here you can see a description of the  hardware environment i'll be using in  this demo  i'll be using three intel next unit of  computing nodes  or intel nux i've imaged each of these  to be running ubuntu 20.04.1  server there are a few linux  distributions which meet the current  requirements for anthocon bare metal  be sure to check the documentation for  the latest operating system and hardware  requirements  i've upgraded two of my nux to have 32  gigabytes of ram  each these will be the nodes where the  kubernetes cluster and therefore any  workloads will  be run the other nook is to be used as  an  admin workstation this can be any linux  workstation  i'll use this node to ssh into the  others to do any configuration  and to run the bmctl command which is  the command line tool for anthocon bare  metal  there are also a number of prerequisites  to set up on the google cloud side  you will need a project and sufficient  privileges on that project  to configure anthocon bare metal you  will also need to configure a few  service accounts for things like logging  and monitoring  and to connect your bare metal cluster  to anthos in gcp  now let's take a look at the process to  set up an anthocon bare metal cluster on  this configuration in order to create a  cluster for anthocon bare metal  we'll need a yaml file to describe our  kubernetes cluster  we can create a convenient base file  using the command  bmctl create config  dash c and giving it a cluster name  there are several components that will  need updating in the created file  i've already created one and configured  it for my environment  let's take a closer look  if you're familiar with kubernetes yaml  manifests  this probably looks familiar to you  at the top of this file we have a  variety of key file paths  which we will need for cluster  installation then we begin blocks  declaring api objects  the first interesting one here is the  cluster definition  you can see here that we can define a  cluster type  anthocon bare metal offers various  architecture configurations  that can be useful in multi-cluster  environments or  single cluster environments such as at  the edge  we will use gke connect to register this  cluster with  anthos  here we define the information for our  cluster's control plane  in my case as this is just a demo  environment  i will be using a single machine for my  control plane for  high availability you would generally  want three  here i have the ip my admin workstation  will use to connect to the control plane  node  next i need to specify the ip ranges  kubernetes will use to create pods and  services there is also a load balancer  component needed for anthocon bare metal  this can run on the control plane nodes  or as a separate machine  or set of machines in my demo  environment i will be using my control  plane node also  as my load balancer so here i specify a  virtual ip on my network  to be used for the load balancer i also  specify a range of ips for use by the  load balancer to expose my services  if you're using a proxy you will need to  add some configuration here  i'm not so i'll leave this all commented  out here we see some information used  for logging in metrics  and here at the bottom of the file is  the definition of our worker node pool  the machines in this pool are where  workloads will actually be run  as i mentioned in my case i have only  one  now i'm going to use bmctl the antho  spare metal command line tool  to deploy the cluster i've defined to do  that i'll run  bmctl create  cluster dash c and give it a cluster  name  this will take a few minutes to complete  now we've successfully deployed our  cluster  we can confirm that we can connect to  our cluster using cubectl  we can see in the output of our cluster  creation the cubeconfig file we'll need  to use to connect the cluster  running this command  [Music]  shows me the nodes of my two node demo  cluster i'll also go ahead and export my  cube config  so i don't have to specify it in each  command  the bare metal command line tool not  only created the cluster  but also connected the cluster to anthos  in google cloud  so we should be able to see our cluster  in the anthos dashboard  in the google cloud console as well  here in the google cloud console we will  go to the anthos dashboard  and here in the anthos dashboard you can  see that in the cluster status box  it lists one healthy cluster if we click  view clusters we can see our cluster  here  this is the cluster we just created on a  set of intel noc hardware  and now we can see and manage it in  google cloud  now let's deploy an app to our cluster  for this demo  i'm going to use a very simple hello  world app  if we look at our deployment yaml file  you can see that i'm defining a  kubernetes deployment here  with three replicas and using a hello  app  container image  [Music]  now to create my deployment i'll run  cubectl  create dash f and give it my deployment  file  [Music]  you can see that the deployment has been  created now we can check it with cube  ctl  get deploy you can see our deployment is  up  with three ready pods now i'll create a  service  using the myservice.yaml file  you can see that this is a service of  type load balancer which we'll be able  to reach  on port 80. we'll create this with  cubectl  create dashf  and give it the file name and we can  take a look at our service with cube ctl  get svc or service using my services  external ip  provided by the load balancer i can run  a curl command on that ip to see that my  app is running properly  here you can see in our output that my  application is running as i expect  in this demo we walked through the  requirements to create an anthocon bare  metal cluster  saw some of the benefits you get from  anthocyan google cloud  and deployed an application to our  on-prem cluster  check out google cloud for more on  running enterprise grade  hybrid and multi-cloud architectures  with anthos  you
 

